<Window x:Class="FastApp.PaletteWindow"
        xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
        xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
        xmlns:wv2="clr-namespace:Microsoft.Web.WebView2.Wpf;assembly=Microsoft.Web.WebView2.Wpf"
        Title="FastApp"
        Width="820" Height="560"
        WindowStyle="None"
        ResizeMode="NoResize"
        ShowInTaskbar="False"
        Topmost="True"
        WindowStartupLocation="Manual">

    <!-- Manual, not CenterScreen. WPF applies a startup location once, at the
         first Show, and overrides whatever Left/Top were set beforehand. The
         first Show here is the prewarm, which is supposed to be invisible.
         So the prewarm's attempt to park the window off the desktop was thrown
         away and every launch put this on screen instead. ShowPalette places it
         now, which is the only place that ever wanted a position.

         Deliberately no AllowsTransparency and no Effect on this subtree.
         Either one puts the window on WPF's layered / software rendering path,
         and a WebView2 composited through DirectComposition then draws nothing
         at all: the window appears, correctly sized, completely black. That is
         exactly what happened on the first attempt here.

         The rounded corners and the shadow come from DWM instead, set in
         OnSourceInitialized, which is also where every other Windows 11 app gets
         them. -->
    <wv2:WebView2 x:Name="Web"/>
</Window>
